Z||000002||FieroSoul||07-26-2001||07:06 AM||||  I have heard of bad alternators squealing like this too.  Maybe the belts are okay but your bearings are gone.||||reg||3
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||07-26-2001||10:41 AM||||are you checking the play up inthe engine compartment, or underneight the car, where the belt spans the longest distance.<BR> <BR>  tight is a relative term - the best way I can describe a tight belt is when you deflect it in the center of a 12 or 15" span then a loose belt feels like a really loose belt - you can deflect it easily and then it reaches a point where all of a sudden its like you hit something going sideways. try it sometime when the adjusting bolt is backed off, as you pull the alt tight the distance decreases for the 'slop' range.<P>  a tight belt is actually streached - like a rubber band - I always tighten my belts up till they are just starting to streach.<P>  think of it this way - if the belt has 3" of slop it is not streached, if it has 1" it is not streached, if it has 1/10th of an inch of slop it is still not streached - so from a friction standpoint 1/10th" of slop is as bad as 3".<P>  to work without squealing the belt needs to be slightly streached when adjusted - too much is not good cause it puts alot of side load on the alt bearings.<P>  if its still squealing crawl under the car and check for the loose feel there. If you suspect the alt bearings are bad, pull the belt off the alt and give the alt a spin - you will know right away if the bearings are chewed up.||||reg||10
Z||000004||HummelHund||07-26-2001||11:11 AM||||If you're sure it's not the alternator after checking on that, try using a lock nut (isn't that what they're called?) along with a new bolt from a parts store, just make sure it's long enough to poke out the other side of the hole where the normal alternator bolt would go (I got mine at autozone). Then, while someone else pries on the alternator to keep the belt tight, put the bolt through, and, using much patience and lots of acrobatics (you may have to break your fingers, put the bolt on the end of the nut and tighten down. A ratcheting wrench helps here. I did this to my '84 a while back (using Dayco, those Kelly Springfields aren't worth messing with) and I haven't had a problem since.<BR>Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
